\frametitle{Boundary Conditions: Indexing Pattern}

    We found a structural pattern that simplifies boundary condition (BC) logic.

    \begin{itemize}
        \item Let \textbf{$x_0$} be the solving direction.
        \item Let \textbf{$x_1$} and \textbf{$x_2$} be the boundary plane directions, with indices \textbf{$i_1$} and \textbf{$i_2$}.
    \end{itemize}

    \vspace{1em}

    The component type (Normal vs. Tangential) depends \textbf{only on the parity} of the boundary indices:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Normal Component:} $i_1$ is \textbf{even} AND $i_2$ is \textbf{even}.
        \item \textbf{Tangential Component:} $i_1$ is \textbf{odd} OR $i_2$ is \textbf{odd}.
    \end{itemize}

    \vspace{1em}

    This pattern (visualized below) allows for highly efficient and readable code, replacing complex checks with a simple `if` statement.


    \begin{figure}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\textwidth, keepaspectratio]{img/3dGrid.png}
        \caption{Grid visualizing the even/odd pattern.}
    \end{figure}

